  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through next Friday) \nIssued at 1034 PM CDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\nOur next upper level system will be a decently amplified upper \ntrough pushing eastward across the Central Rockies. This system will \nlikely bring more wind than it will rainfall. A surface low will \ndevelop over the foothills of Colorado Sunday and will help for \nsurface winds to increase to 20-25 mph out of the south. With upper \nflow remaining out of the southwest and the bulk of upper lift \nstaying well to our north, the region will likely be dryslotted sans \na few isolated showers. The upper low will pass to our northeast by \nTuesday and will usher in a weak cold front with highs only cooling \n(possibly) into the 60s, down from the 70s on Monday. This will be \ndue to lack of a fetch of cool surface temps to transport southward. \nWeak upper ridging will move in mid week and eventually replaced \nwith mostly benign, zonal flow through late week.",
